%S A400207 123456789,123856789,126456789,126856789,129456789,129856789,
%T A400207 143456789,143856789,146456789,146856789,149456789,149856789,
%U A400207 163456789,163856789,166456789,166856789,169456789,169856789,183456789,183856789,186456789,186856789,189456789,189856789,223456789
%N A400207 Sparkly numbers: a positive integer is called sparkly if it has exactly 9 digits, and for any m between 1 and 9 (inclusive), the m-th digit is a positive multiple of m.
%C A400207 The 2019 New Zealand Mathematical Olympiad, Round Two, Problem 1, asks how many positive integers are sparkly? The official solution gives the answer 216.
%C A400207 In fact, there are 9, 4, 3 and 2 choices for the first four digits, respectively, and therefore there are 9*4*3*2 = 216 sparkly numbers in total, with a(216) = 989856789.
%C A400207 See the official problem and detailed solution in link.
%C A400207 Since the fifth, sixth, seventh, eighth and ninth digits must be positive multiples of 5, 6, 7, 8 and 9 respectively, they are necessarily 5, 6, 7, 8 and 9. Hence every sparkly number ends in 56789.
%C A400207 The only sparkly number that is zeroless pandigital is 123456789, first term of A050289.
%H A400207 Michael S. Branicky, <a href="/A400207/b400207.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..216</a>
%H A400207 New Zealand Mathematical Olympiad Committee, <a href="https://www.mathsolympiad.org.nz/competitions/nzmo/files/problems.php?round=2&amp;year=2019&amp;type=solutions">2019 NZMO Round 2, problem 1</a>.
%H A400207 <a href="/index/O#Olympiads">Index to sequences related to Olympiads and other Mathematical Competitions</a>.
%e A400207 269856789 is sparkly, since: 1|2, 2|6, 3|9, 4|8, 5|5, 6|6, 7|7, 8|8, and 9|9.
%o A400207 (Python)
%o A400207 from itertools import product
%o A400207 afull = [int("".join(p)) for p in product("123456789", "2468", "369", "48", ["56789"])]
%o A400207 print(afull[:25]) # _Michael S. Branicky_, Sep 21 2026
%o A400207 (PARI) seq() = res=List(); forvec(x=vector(9, i, [1, 9\i]), listput(res, fromdigits(vector(9, i, x[i]*i)))); res \\ _David A. Corneth_, Sep 21 2026
